{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MCsqFaRS3Z9bAXMSkTR2h7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016SBVHkQkrDLgfDXTKW2X5T","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Research MLE rounds anti-scaling","subagent_type":"Explore","prompt":"I'm investigating why the MLE Rounds phase in the GPU prover takes LONGER at APC 300 (174ms) than at APC 0 (114ms) despite APC 300 having far fewer cells. The issue seems to be per-AIR overhead with 623 AIR instances at APC 300 vs 99 at APC 0.\n\nI need to understand the MLE rounds code path in the GPU prover:\n1. The main MLE round loop — where it iterates over AIRs or traces\n2. What per-AIR or per-trace work happens in each round\n3. The batched kernels that were already implemented (zerocheck_batch_mle_kernel, logup_batch_mle_kernel, batched_nondegen_mle_round_kernel)\n4. What CPU-side work remains per-AIR/per-trace between kernel launches\n5. Any sequential operations that can't be batched\n\nSearch in:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/` — look for files related to MLE, sumcheck, logup_zerocheck_mle\n- `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/` — the CUDA kernels\n\nI need file paths, line numbers, and the full loop structure to understand what creates per-AIR overhead in MLE rounds. Report the call chain from the MLE round entry point through all sub-operations."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6641,"cache_read_input_tokens":60909,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6641},"output_tokens":57,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f93534bb-5de3-4083-9e85-ad533ac466ab","uuid":"07be47ac-0df0-4287-b23f-138b82874222"}
